"\section=1"
"\name=mailmap"
"\summary=update git repository .mailmap from identity register"

namespace mailmap
{
  {
    "<options> <identities> <dir>",

    "\h|SYNOPSIS|

     \c{\b{mailmap} [<options>] <identities> [<dir>]}

     \h|DESCRIPTION|

     The \cb{mailmap} command updates the \cb{.mailmap} file of a git
     repository so that it maps every identity (name and email) the
     repository history carries to the preferred identity of the person it
     belongs to, as recorded in the identity register <identities> (see
     IDENTITY REGISTER below for details). The history
     identities are the commit authors and the \cb{Co-authored-by} trailer
     values.

     If <dir> is specified, then it should be a directory inside the working
     tree of the repository to update or, with \cb{--all}, the directory to
     search for repositories. Otherwise, the current working directory is
     assumed.

     The \cb{.mailmap} is generated as a whole and replaces the existing one,
     which means that changes to it belong in the identity register. It
     contains an entry for each history identity that differs from the
     preferred identity of its person, with the entries sorted, so the same
     history and register always produce the same file. Before anything is
     changed, git itself confirms that the generated \cb{.mailmap} maps every
     history identity to its person (see \cb{git-check-mailmap(1)}).

     The update then proceeds as follows:

     \ol|

     \li|Prepare every repository: generate its \cb{.mailmap} and establish
     that it can be installed, committed, and, if requested, pushed. If any
     repository fails to prepare, then nothing is changed in any of them.|

     \li|Print the plan: for each repository whose \cb{.mailmap} is not up to
     date, the number of history identities, where the commit is pushed to,
     and the changes to the \cb{.mailmap}.|

     \li|Ask for confirmation, unless \cb{--yes} is specified.|

     \li|Install each \cb{.mailmap} with a single rename, then commit and,
     if requested, push it.||

     For example, to update and commit the \cb{.mailmap} of the repository
     in the current working directory:

     \
     $ mailmap identities

     repository:         /src/hello
     history identities: 3 (3 mapped)

     --- a/.mailmap
     +++ b/.mailmap
     @@ -1 +1,3 @@
      # Generated from the identity register by mailmap.
     +Jane Doe <jane@example.org> <jdoe@example.com>
     +Jane Doe <jane@example.org> J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>

     update '/src/hello/.mailmap'? [y/n]: y
     \

     The commit subject is \cb{Update .mailmap} and its message body lists the
     entries the update adds and removes, for example:

     \
     Update .mailmap

       add    Jane Doe <jane@example.org> <jdoe@example.com>
       remove Jane Doe <jane@example.org> <jane@gone.example.org>
     \

     Note that the committer becomes one of the history identities and so the
     identity register must attribute them for the next update to succeed.

     To update, commit, and push the \cb{.mailmap} of every repository in the
     \cb{org/} directory, confirming once for all of them:

     \
     $ mailmap --all --push identities org/
     \

     \h#identity-register|IDENTITY REGISTER|

     The identity register is a manifest list (see
     \l{bpkg#manifest-format Manifest Format}) with one manifest per person.
     For example:

     \
     : 1
     name: Jane Doe
     email: jane@example.org
     alias: J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
     alias: <jdoe@example.com>
     :
     name: John Roe
     email: john@example.org
     \

     The \cb{name} and \cb{email} values specify the preferred identity of the
     person and appear once. The \cb{alias} values specify the other
     identities of the person and can appear any number of times. An alias in
     the \c{\i{name} <\i{email}>} form matches the identity with this name and
     email and an alias in the \c{<\i{email}>} form matches every identity
     with this email. The \cb{authors} value specifies how the person is
     listed in \cb{AUTHORS} (see \cb{authors(1)}) and \cb{mailmap} only
     checks it. Any other value is an error.

     An identity is matched the same way git applies a \cb{.mailmap} entry to
     it: an alias with a name takes precedence and names and emails compare
     case-insensitively. The register must attribute each identity to at most
     one person, which holds if:

     \ul|

     \li|An email, preferred or aliased, belongs to one person.|

     \li|A name at an email belongs to one person.|

     \li|A name alias matches no other person's preferred identity.|

     \li|An alias is not covered by its person's own emails.||

     Note that the email alias of one person and the name alias of another may
     refer to the same email. This is how two people who committed with a
     shared email are told apart.

     Every history identity must be attributed. The command fails if the
     register does not attribute some of them, listing each one, for example:

     \
     error: history of '/src/hello' has identities not in 'identities'
       info: Stranger <stranger@example.net>
     \
     "
  }

  class options
  {
    "\h|OPTIONS|"

    bool --all|-a
    {
      "Operate on every repository found under \i{dir}. Without this option,
       the repository \i{dir} is in is operated on. The search does not
       descend into a repository working tree, so nested repositories
       (submodules, repositories checked out inside another one) are never
       visited. Linked worktrees are skipped as well, since their repository
       is visited through its main working tree. Hidden directories are
       searched."
    };

    bool --no-commit
    {
      "Don't commit the updated \cb{.mailmap}. By default, the \cb{.mailmap}
       is committed and only it is, whatever else is staged."
    };

    bool --push
    {
      "Push the commit to the upstream of the current branch. To push exactly
       this commit, the branch must not be ahead of its upstream before the
       update. If the push fails, then the commit is kept and the rest of the
       repositories are still updated."
    };

    bool --show-push
    {
      "Print the push command without executing it. This allows examining
       the commit before pushing it. Note that the command is printed to
       \cb{stdout}."
    };

    bool --print-only
    {
      "Print to \cb{stdout} what would be done without actually doing
       anything."
    };

    bool --yes|-y
    {
      "Don't prompt for confirmation before updating the \cb{.mailmap}."
    };

    bool --help
    {
      "Print usage information and exit."
    };

    bool --version
    {
      "Print version and exit."
    };
  };

  "
  \h|EXIT STATUS|

  Non-zero exit status is returned in case of an error, including when the
  update is not confirmed. Nothing is changed in any repository if the error
  is diagnosed before the confirmation.
  "
}
